  {
   "k": "1dbqrdy",
   "en": "A path between two adjacent provinces is the straight line between their centers. It is traced on a pixel grid⟦0⟧, making diagonal lines noticeably longer than north-south and east-west lines. The terrain along this path (along with its distance) determines its movement cost.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "dait36",
   "en": "The path may consist of the source and target province's terrain in varying proportions or even different terrains altogether. For example the path between two coastal mountain provinces at a fjord might consist of 40% mountain and 60% ocean, resulting in ⟦0⟧ movement cost for this path instead of ⟦1⟧ in a pure mountain range.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "1e7rp10",
   "en": "The path also determines whether direction-dependent terrain features apply."
  },
  {
   "k": "1sukoci",
   "en": "The path cost of amphibious landings is fixed at 24⟦0⟧ pixels (170.736 km).⟦1⟧",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "18o9b59",
   "en": "Terrain features"
  },
  {
   "k": "1073e7f",
   "en": "Terrain features can apply adjusters and modifiers in addition to the base terrain. They stack multiplicatively with base terrain values. Unlike other terrain modifiers, they only affect the attacker because many depend on the specific path the attackers take, and the defender might fight against attackers from different directions. Attack adjusters apply to the attacker's soft and hard attack, and the defense adjusters apply to their breakthrough."
  },
  {
   "k": "1syzsdc",
   "en": "⟦0⟧ ⟦1⟧ to attack and defense and ⟦2⟧ to movement.",
   "frags": 3
  },
  {
   "k": "lp602s",
   "en": "Small river:"
  },
  {
   "k": "1ex3zng",
   "en": "Large river:"
